Transcript:\n- Good afternoon and\na warm welcome to you, our in-house 
audience,\nand to the many viewers who are watching us on Zoom. My name is 
Claire Lyons\nand I'm the curator of antiquities here at\nthe Villa and the 
curator of the new exhibition now\non view on the second floor, “The Kingdom of 
Pylos:\nWarrior-Princes of Mycenaean Greece.” Today is a celebration for us. The
villa reopened to the public yesterday and this is our...\n(audience applauds) 
Yeah. This is our first in-person program after the Palisades fire 
swept\nthrough six months ago. Local friends and neighbors will find the 
surrounding area much\nchanged from the absence of over 1,400 trees that\nwere 
cut on this property. But fortunately, our building and collection were 
safeguarded,\nand it's truly wonderful to see visitors here once again. 
Recognizing the Gabrieleño\nTongva people whom we honor as the original 
caretakers\nof this place known as Tovaangar, planning is already underway to 
reimagine the site's\nnative environment, its landscapes, and vistas. This 
afternoon's lectures\ncelebrate the reopened villa as well as a landmark 
exhibition. The Mycenaeans have often\nbeen glimpsed through the lens of Homer's
\"Iliad\" and \"Odyssey,\" the two famous epics of the\nTrojan War and its 
aftermath. Agamemnon, king of Mycenae, which gave Mycenaean\nculture its modern 
name, and warriors like Odysseus and Achilles color our\nimagination of 
prehistoric Greece. Among those heroes was\nthe legendary King Nestor, who sent 
the second largest fleet to Troy and returned safely to\nhis homeland in Pylos. 
Through the objects on display, our aim is to reveal the\nhistory behind those 
myths, and the science behind some\nspectacular discoveries. This is not just 
the first\nexhibition to focus on Pylos and ancient Messenia, a region\nin the 
southwest Peloponnese, it is also the first major\ndisplay of Mycenaean culture 
and art outside of Europe, and it features over 230 finds, many of\nwhich have 
not been shown to the public before. We are grateful indeed to\nthe Ministry of 
Culture in Greece for the opportunity to bring this unique material to Los 
Angeles, and to the archeologists\nwhose exciting research has advanced our 
understanding\nof the Greek Bronze Age. Two of them are with us today. Let me 
