sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia*.[^1]
- Author: Arthur J. Evans.[^1]
- Place and date of publication: London, 1925.[^1]
- Content structure:
  - Section I (pp. 1–42): *“Sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and 
bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”*.[^1]
  - Section II (pp. 43–75): *“A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world”*.[^1]
  - Plates I–V with illustrations.[^1]

Within the work (via the downloadable PDF), Evans:

- Describes a hoard of gold signet rings and bead seals from a Mycenaean chamber
tomb near Thisbe in Boeotia, discovered under non‑official circumstances during 
World War I by a local peasant, later reaching the antiquities market and being 
acquired by Evans.[^1]
- Identifies one especially elaborate gold signet ring as the “Ring of Nestor,” 
associating it with the Homeric figure Nestor as a literary label rather than a 
precise historical attribution.[^1]
- Provides measurements, discussion of the ring’s gold composition, form of the 
hoop and bezel, and compares its workmanship with other Mycenaean gold rings 
from Kakóvatos and elsewhere.[^1]
- Analyses the iconography on the bezel: a complex scene involving an altar or 
sacred structure, tree or vegetation, and multiple human and possibly divine 
figures, which he interprets as a vision of a Minoan “after‑world,” relating it 
to Minoan religious beliefs and funerary symbolism.[^1]
- Chronologically places the hoard, including the ring, in the Late Mycenaean 
period while emphasising strong Minoan stylistic influences.[^1]
- Discusses the tomb context in Boeotia as evidence of Mycenaean cultural 
connections with Crete and the diffusion of Minoan religious imagery into 
mainland Greece.[^1]

