      "document_summary": "Digitized edition of Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph
on the Ring of Nestor and a hoard of gold signet rings and bead seals from a 
Mycenaean chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia. The work is divided into a 
section on the sepulchral treasure and a section interpretively titled “A 
glimpse into the Minoan after‑world,” and includes five plates. Evans describes 
the discovery of the hoard under non‑official conditions during World War I, its
acquisition, and its context in a rock‑cut tomb near the village of Dombrena. He
provides detailed descriptions and measurements of the gold rings and 
bead‑seals, highlighting one especially elaborate signet ring that he labels the
“Ring of Nestor.” He analyses its gold composition, hoop and bezel form, and 
compares its craftsmanship with other Mycenaean gold rings, including those from
Kakóvatos. Evans gives a detailed account of the bezel’s iconography, 
interpreting a multi‑figure ritual scene with an altar or sacred structure, 
vegetation, and human and possibly divine figures as a vision of a Minoan 
afterlife and as evidence of Minoan religious imagery adopted in a Late 
Mycenaean context. He dates the assemblage to the Late Mycenaean period, 
emphasising strong Minoan stylistic influence, and uses the tomb context to 
argue for cultural connections between mainland Greece and Crete.",
