    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Vlachopoulos (2020) presents a comprehensive study of
the Ring of Nestor as one of the largest Aegean gold signet rings, providing 
specific measurements and ranking it among known Aegean rings. He reconstructs 
the discovery context and history of publication, including Arthur Evans’ early 
interpretation and later scholarly doubts about authenticity. The chapter 
reports results of recent contextual and metallurgical analyses that support the
ring’s authenticity as a genuine Late Bronze Age Aegean signet ring. Stylistic 
comparison situates the ring in a Late Minoan or Mycenaean palatial milieu, with
strong Minoan stylistic and technical traits but a Mycenaean mainland elite 
context of use (Tiryns treasure, association with Nestor). The iconography is 
analyzed as a complex multi-register religious scene featuring a seated female 
figure beneath a tree, approaching male figures, animals, and secondary figures,
interpreted within elite religious and ritual ideology. The ring is discussed as
both a functional signet for sealing and an elite heirloom (keimēlion) with 
roles in memory, identity, and transmission of religious imagery. The chapter 
summarizes several scholarly debates, notably about authenticity, the nature of 
the central female figure and the type of ritual or narrative depicted, and the 
relative weight of Minoan versus Mycenaean elements in the ring’s style and 
imagery.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Palmer’s 2014 doctoral thesis surveys Late Bronze Age
Aegean glyptic and includes the Ring of Nestor as a case study. The ring is 
framed within a Late Bronze Age, specifically Late Helladic II–III, 
chronological scheme and associated with Mycenaean Tiryns, drawing on earlier 
scholarship such as Arthur Evans and CMS publications. The thesis examines the 
ring’s iconography within a broader repertoire of religious and ritual scenes on
seals, including tree cult, goddess worship, and processions. Palmer highlights 
Minoan stylistic traits in the imagery and composition while emphasizing that 
many seals used by Mycenaean elites were either Minoan-made or Minoanizing in 
style. The ring’s function is discussed in terms of its role as a signet for 
administrative sealing and as a high-prestige status object, probably preserved 
as an heirloom within a treasure deposit. The thesis notes ongoing scholarly 
disagreements over the interpretation of the scene on the Ring of Nestor, 
including whether it represents epiphany, mortuary ritual, or generic cult 
imagery.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The Biblioteka Nauki article on metal and Late Bronze
Age Aegean signet rings examines gold rings as material and social objects and 
uses the Ring of Nestor as a key example. It cites Arthur Evans’ 1925 study, 
summarizing his view that the ring’s imagery relates to a Minoan afterlife or 
\"after‑world\" and that it formed part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet 
rings and bead seals from a burial context in Boeotia, connected to Mycenaean 
funerary practices. The article accepts a Late Bronze Age date for the ring and 
discusses its placement in Mycenaean funerary or elite ritual contexts while 
entertaining the possibility of Minoan manufacture followed by Mycenaean 
deposition. It attributes the iconography to Minoan religious traditions, such 
as tree cult, processions, divine figures, and composite creatures, and analyses
Mycenaean reception and reinterpretation of such images in mainland settings, 
especially in relation to beliefs about death and ancestor veneration. The 
article highlights scholarly debate over whether the imagery should be primarily
interpreted within Minoan religious frameworks or as modified by later Mycenaean
understandings.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The article on Mycenaean sphag- (slaughter, blood, 
sacrifice) investigates sacrificial concepts by combining textual evidence with 
iconographic material, including glyptic. It references Vlachopoulos’ 2020 study
of the Ring of Nestor and treats the ring as an example of Mycenaean religious 
iconography. The ring is used to explore whether its figures, animals, and tree 
may relate to sacrificial practices or ritual processions, situating it in a 
Late Bronze Age Mycenaean ritual context. The article presents the Ring of 
Nestor as part of a broader corpus of Mycenaean ritual scenes and notes disputes
about whether such depictions represent specific rites, including sacrifice, or 
more generalized symbolic or ideological imagery. It thereby contributes to 
debates on the function of the ring’s imagery and its relationship to actual 
ritual practice.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The Heidelberg Propylaeum chapter on composite 
creatures in Aegean glyptic considers fixed hybrids such as dragons, griffins, 
and sphinxes, and includes the Ring of Nestor as an example. It describes a 
\"Minoan Dragon\" on the ring depicted in a natural setting, standing on grassy 
ground before a tree trunk with branches above, and analyzes the creature’s role
in the composition. The chapter interprets this hybrid as related to themes of 
protection and guardianship of sacred trees and liminal zones between human and 
divine realms. It assigns the ring’s imagery to Minoan religious traditions, 
noting the typicality of composite creatures in Minoan glyptic and frescoes and 
their adoption into Mycenaean iconography. The chapter discusses cultural 
