guardian symbols, decorative motifs, or emblems of divine presence.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "Nilsson’s monograph on the Mycenaean origin of Greek 
mythology uses archaeological evidence, including glyptic and signet rings, to 
argue for continuity between Mycenaean beliefs and later Greek myth. It cites 
Arthur Evans’ article on the Ring of Nestor and adopts Evans’ dating of the ring
to the Mycenaean period, and its inclusion in a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet rings and bead seals. Nilsson treats the Ring of Nestor’s elaborate scene
as potentially reflecting mythological narratives or proto‑mythic religious 
motifs that anticipated later Homeric or Greek stories. He recognizes that the 
scene may instead depict generic cult imagery and notes the difficulty of 
securely identifying specific myths. The ring is presented as evidence for early
conceptions of gods, heroes, and the afterlife in the Mycenaean world, while 
acknowledging interpretive uncertainty.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d7",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu article on CMS I 179 of the Tiryns 
treasure focuses on a famous Mycenaean signet ring and relates it to the Ring of
Nestor, discussing their identification or conflation in earlier scholarship. It
characterizes the ring as a keimēlion, underscoring its role as an heirloom 
important for ancestral respect and Mycenaean cultural identity. The study 
analyzes the ring’s place in the Tiryns treasure, interpreted as a deposit of 
high-prestige objects, possibly sepulchral or ritual. Chronologically, the ring 
is dated to the Mycenaean palatial period, Late Helladic III, based on stylistic
and technical criteria and the associated finds. Cultural attribution is 
described as Mycenaean in terms of ownership and deposition but with strong 
Minoan stylistic features in the iconography and craftsmanship. The article 
addresses scholarly disagreements over whether the treasure represents funerary 
deposits, temple offerings, or a collection of heirlooms, and discusses how 
these differing views affect interpretations of the Ring of Nestor’s function 
