      "source_ref": "s7"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d8",
      "document_summary": "The Cambridge book chapter on Aegean art in the final
palatial period at Knossos surveys Late Minoan III art, including seals and 
signet rings, and mentions the Ring of Nestor (catalogued as CMS VI 277) as a 
ring with an extraordinary religious scene. It describes the multi-figure 
composition featuring a central tree, a seated female figure, an approaching 
male figure, animals, and possibly composite creatures, emphasizing its 
complexity and ritual character. Based on stylistic analysis and comparison with
contemporary Cretan material, the chapter attributes the ring to a Knossian 
