early Mycenaean chronological horizon, suggesting Minoan palatial production 
followed by entry into a Mycenaean mainland treasure deposit. The chapter 
reviews scholarly interpretations that see the imagery as a religious scene, 
possibly a goddess epiphany or cult ceremony, and notes disagreement over 
whether the scene should be understood as mythological narrative or purely 
cultic representation. It highlights the ring’s significance for studying 
cross-regional exchange and the transmission of religious imagery and elite 
symbolism between Knossos and Mycenaean centers.",
      "source_ref": "s8"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d9",
      "document_summary": "The Royal Holloway PhD thesis on Mycenaean colonies, 
trading posts, and myth-related archaeology references Arthur Evans’ 1925 
article on the Ring of Nestor and Evelyn Vermeule’s work. It treats the ring as 
a Mycenaean-period object and underscores Evans’ interpretation of it as part of
a sepulchral treasure, used as evidence for Mycenaean beliefs concerning death 
and the afterlife. The thesis supports a Mycenaean date by linking the ring’s 
context and stylistic features with other Late Bronze Age material and situating
it within Mycenaean colonies and trade networks. It notes that Evans strongly 
emphasized the Minoan character of the imagery, whereas later scholarship sees 
the ring as reflecting a more complex interplay between Minoan and Mycenaean 
cultural spheres, and it acknowledges continued debate about the precise balance
