original may differ.", "paraphrase"]}, {"id": "20260628151100324553_acd5c2fc", 
"type": "Document", "source_id": "20260628151100055197_4068b239", "text": 
"Cain’s 2001 American Journal of Archaeology article, \"Deconstructing a 
Narrative of Epiphany on the Isopata Ring,\" primarily re-examines the 
interpretation of an epiphany scene on the Isopata Ring but also includes 
comparative material, among which is the Ring of Nestor. The article reproduces 
drawings of impressions of several gold rings with epiphany-type scenes, one of 
which is identified as the Ring of Nestor, citing Evans’ earlier discussion. In 
the course of analyzing how epiphany narratives have been constructed by 
scholars, Cain describes the composition of these scenes, including the 
arrangement of human figures, divine or supernatural appearances, and cult 
equipment. The Ring of Nestor is used as one of the examples in evaluating 
whether such scenes genuinely depict epiphanies or whether previous 
reconstructions overstate the narrative coherence of the imagery."}, {"id": 
"20260628151102180138_f4c16ddc", "type": "Evidence", "evidence_type": "other", 
"confidence": "low", "claim": "Within its analysis, the paper discusses the 
figural composition on the ring’s bezel, including the number and type of 
figures, associated symbols such as altars, trees, animals, or celestial signs, 
and the arrangement of these elements into a narrative or ritual scene.", 
"qualifiers": [], "source_id": "20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "text": "Within 
its analysis, the paper discusses the figural composition on the ring’s bezel, 
including the number and type of figures, associated symbols such as altars, 
trees, animals, or celestial signs, and the arrangement of these elements into a
narrative or ritual scene.", "evidence_key": 
"evidence:0c244f988a6fa582249ea019", "document_id": 
"20260628151100215425_d71e706d", "status": "pending", "limitations": ["The 
specific motifs mentioned here are drawn from typical Aegean iconographic 
discussions and are not individually confirmed for this ring in the snippet; 
this part involves some inference about likely content based on the paper’s 
topic.", "inference"]}, {"id": "20260628151100253098_2323e9b2", "type": 
"Document", "source_id": "20260628151059991695_781fe51a", "text": "The Aegis 
volume \"ICON. Art and Meaning in Aegean Seal Images\" is an edited collection 
on Aegean glyptic art and its interpretation. Within this broader context, the 
volume references Arthur Evans’ classic article on the Ring of Nestor, titled 
\"The Ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan afterworld and a sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet rings from Thisbe, Boeotia\" in JHS 45. The discussion 
places the Ring of Nestor among gold signet rings from Thisbe in Boeotia and 
treats it as part of a group of Late Bronze Age Aegean seal images that 
illuminate religious or funerary beliefs. The ring is used as a case study for 
how seal iconography can be read in terms of art, meaning, and symbolic content,
especially in relation to concepts such as the Minoan afterlife and ritual 
