"20260628151100401174_3b8d3dfe", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628151100109801_853bf17c", "text": "The Dartmouth Aegean Prehistoric 
Archaeology course bibliography for Lesson 14 lists key scholarly works relevant
to narrative imagery and related topics in Aegean prehistory, including 
publications on the Ring of Nestor. Among the cited works is Arthur Evans’ 
article \"The ‘Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-world\" in JHS 
45. The bibliography indicates that this article, along with later 
re-evaluations by other scholars, is central to teaching about the Ring of 
Nestor and its iconography. The page serves primarily as a bibliographic guide 
rather than providing detailed descriptions or interpretations of the ring 
itself."}, {"id": "20260628151100215425_d71e706d", "type": "Document", 
"source_id": "20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "text": "The paper titled \"Some 
observations on the iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'\" concentrates on the 
imagery of the Ring of Nestor and its place within Aegean iconography, 
especially in relation to issues of forgery and authenticity. It offers a 
detailed visual description of the ring’s scene, analyzing the number, type, and
posture of the human or anthropomorphic figures, the presence of symbolic motifs
such as altars, trees, animals, or mythological beings, and the arrangement of 
these elements into a coherent composition. The author compares the ring’s 
iconographic program with known Minoan and Mycenaean religious scenes, assessing
whether the composition aligns with established epiphany or ritual imagery or 
instead seems artificially composite in a way that might suggest modern 
manufacture. The study thus uses iconographic analysis as a tool to argue for a 
particular position in the authenticity debate surrounding the ring."}, {"id": 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205", "type": "Source", "source_type": "paper", 
"title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity in …”", "url": 
