"type": "Document", "source_id": "20260628151100082676_286f4fca", "text": "The 
article \"Traces of Places: Sacred Sites in Miniature on Minoan Gold Rings\" 
examines how Minoan gold rings depict miniature sacred sites that combine 
architectural features, cult equipment, landscape elements, and mythological 
creatures. In its discussion, the Ring of Nestor is treated as one of the case 
studies. The text notes that on the Ring of Nestor a griffin is associated with 
or seated on a particular structure described as a seat, and relates this motif 
to the concept of a sacred place. The article also discusses table altars, 
proposing that in reality these altars were probably wooden, portable objects 
used in sacrificial rituals, and connects this interpretation to the 
representation of such altars on rings including the Ring of Nestor. Through 
this analysis, the ring’s iconography is interpreted in terms of ritual 
architecture, sacred space, and the roles of animals or mythological beings in 
religious imagery."}, {"id": "20260628151100844212_e1d6305d", "type": 
"Statement", "confidence": "medium", "qualifiers": [], "source_id": 
"20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "text": "The author of \"Some observations on 
the iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’\" evaluates whether the ring’s 
iconographic program coheres with known Minoan and Mycenaean religious scenes, 
using this analysis to argue for a position in the authenticity debate.", 
"statement_key": "statement:95d2a17aa0a7c54e8c19b2bb", "document_id": 
"20260628151100215425_d71e706d", "limitations": ["The specific stance on 
authenticity and detailed comparative examples are not included in the 
snippet-based summary."]}, {"id": "20260628151100986673_cb85539d", "type": 
"Statement", "confidence": "medium", "qualifiers": [], "source_id": 
"20260628151059991695_781fe51a", "text": "In the Aegis volume, the Ring of 
Nestor is treated as a significant example of Aegean seal imagery used to 
explore religious or funerary symbolism on gold rings.", "statement_key": 
"statement:4bdec2ab72506ca412885168", "document_id": 
"20260628151100253098_2323e9b2", "limitations": ["The exact chapters and 
passages where the ring is discussed, and the details of the symbolic 
interpretation, are not specified in the summary."]}, {"id": 
"20260628151100177995_91987f70", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205", "text": "Vlachopoulos’ 2020 chapter focuses on 
the Ring of Nestor within the context of Aegean iconography and debates about 
authenticity. It gives specific measurements for the ring, stating that it 
measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and characterizes it as the fifth largest Aegean 
signet ring. The study situates the ring among Aegean gold signet rings of the 
Late Bronze Age, discussing its material, manufacturing techniques, stylistic 
traits, and how these relate to securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean 
glyptic works. A major theme is the history of scholarly suspicion that the Ring
of Nestor might be a forgery and the more recent body of research that, 
according to the author, confirms its authenticity. The chapter includes 
detailed description and interpretation of the imagery on the ring’s bezel, 
addressing the figures, spatial setting, and possible ritual or religious 
significance, and reassesses earlier iconographic interpretations in light of 
comparative material."}, {"id": "20260628151100638682_7e899ba3", "type": 
"Statement", "confidence": "medium", "qualifiers": [], "source_id": 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205", "text": "According to Vlachopoulos, earlier 
scholarship had suspected that the Ring of Nestor might be a forgery, but more 
recent research, including his own analysis, concludes that the ring is 
authentic.", "statement_key": "statement:7840d59f6242fe78544a6121", 
"document_id": "20260628151100177995_91987f70", "limitations": ["The specific 
arguments and evidence used to support authenticity are not detailed in the 
snippet-based summary."]}, {"id": "20260628151100288266_f06d4e69", "type": 
"Document", "source_id": "20260628151100022629_e8d266c5", "text": "The American 
Journal of Archaeology review of volume 6 of the \"Corpus der minoischen und 
mykenischen Siegel\" comments on selected entries in the standard corpus of 
Minoan and Mycenaean seals. It notes that the Ring of Nestor is included in the 
corpus as item no. 277 and remarks that some scholars still harbor doubts about 
its authenticity. The review contrasts the Ring of Nestor with other suspect 
pieces in the corpus, implying that opinions on authenticity vary among objects.
By mentioning the ring’s corpus number and the persistence of skepticism, the 
review shows that the Ring of Nestor is sufficiently important to be catalogued 
but remains subject to ongoing debate."}, {"id": 
