returned to Greece by the Nobel Foundation, in a ceremony held on Thursday, May 
19, in Stockholm.\n\nThe ring had been found in 1927, during excavations by the 
Italian Archaeological School at the Mycenaean necropolis of Ialyssos, Rhodes, 
among the offerings of Tomb 61. It was kept in the Archaeological Museum of 
Rhodes, as were all findings from systematic excavations on the island. During 
the German Occupation and Italian Rule in the Dodecanese, the ring was stolen 
from the Museum along with hundreds of antiquities, most of them coins and 
jewelry, which are still missing. Investigations to locate them at the time were
fruitless, despite the mobilization of the Archaeological Service and the 
Police.\n\nFollowing the path of illegal trafficking, the gold ring of Rhodes 
