    "triggering_query": "Ring of Nestor gold ring museum collection",
    "relevance_to_query": "Primary publication by Arthur Evans describing the 
discovery, composition, and context of the gold signet rings found in a tomb 
near Thisbê, Boeotia, including the object later called the Ring of Nestor; 
essential for provenance, discovery circumstances, and early history.",
    "raw_content_or_summary": "Heidelberg University Library hosts a complete 
digitized facsimile of Arthur J. Evans' 1925 work \"The ring of Nestor. A 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" (London, 1925). The volume is
divided into two main sections: (I) \"Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings 
and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" (pp. 1–42) and (II) \"A glimpse into the 
Minoan after-world\" (pp. 43–75), followed by plates.\nEvans describes the 
discovery of a hoard of gold rings and bead‑seals in a chamber tomb cut in rock 
near the modern village of Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, dated to the Late 
Mycenaean period. He recounts that the find was made by a local peasant in 1915 
in a Mycenaean rock‑cut tomb, and that the objects came to his attention through
private channels due to wartime conditions. One of the rings, distinguished by 
its elaborate engraved scene, is the piece he labels the \"Ring of Nestor\". 
Evans provides detailed descriptions of the ring's iconography, including 
altars, trees, possibly chthonic or cultic imagery, and connects the scene to 
Minoan and Mycenaean religious conceptions. He also discusses the composition of
the hoard, associated weapons and other finds from the same group of tombs, and 
attempts to date and culturally contextualize the assemblage. While the 
facsimile site itself focuses on bibliographic metadata and access (DOI, URN, 
download links), the digitized pages contain Evans' full narrative of 
provenance: findspot in Thisbê/Dombrena, circumstances of discovery, and his 
role in acquiring and publishing the treasure. The work does not present a 
modern museum catalog entry but is crucial for reconstructing the early history 
and attribution of the Ring of Nestor."
