> # (2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in 
Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.
> 
> [Profile image of Andreas Vlachopoulos] Andreas Vlachopoulos  
> visibility  
> 2,611 views  
> description  
> 36 pages
> 
> ## Abstract
> 
> The many years of research that led to the present paper on the so-called Ring
of Nestor (CMS VI, nο. 277), with the documentation of its authenticity, have 
offered me the opportunity to discuss the cases of erroneous identifications of 
some Creto-Mycenaean gold rings as fakes. This tendency, underpinned by the 
obscure – even legendary – circumstances in which these rings were found, not 
only the Ring of Nestor but also the Ring of Minos, has been reassessed 
completely in favour of their authenticity. This revision has been significantly
reinforced by recent finds of iconographic subjects of comparable resonance 
(wall-paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories, and so on), as well as by more 
thorough study of the techniques and craftsmanship of these unique signet rings 
of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th-15th century BC). The key 
pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor examined in this paper is Evans’s 
“watch-dog” or “dragon”, namely the guardian of the old “tree of the world”, 
whose branches host the most populous scene in Aegean glyptic art. This motif is
here identified as a scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Αge Aegean 
iconography, which kills the “Μinoan Dragon” of the ring and offers a sound 
interpretation of the ring’s iconography.

此外，Academia 的自动“Key takeaways”摘要（由平台的 AI 生成，非论文原文）：

> 1. The Ring of Nestor, measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, is the fifth largest 
Aegean signet ring.  
> 2. Research confirms its authenticity, contrasting earlier claims of forgery 
by scholars in the 1920s.  
> 3. The scorpion, identified as a significant apotropaic symbol, plays a 
crucial role in the ring's iconography.  
> 4. Recent findings enhance the understanding of Aegean glyptic art from the 
Late Bronze Age (16th-15th century BC).  
> 5. This text aims to document the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and 
reassess historical misconceptions.

