[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': "Current evidence on the Ring of 
Nestor establishes a coherent basis for drafting a Wikipedia-style article while
acknowledging unresolved conflicts about authenticity and provenance. Multiple 
modern studies (Vlachopoulos 2020; Hudler 2019; Cambridge 'Fakes and Dubitanda';
Marinatos for the Met) agree that the object is a gold signet ring with complex 
ritual imagery, traditionally dated to the Late Bronze Age and associated with 
Minoan–Mycenaean glyptic. However, scholars are divided between those who treat 
the ring as an authentic ancient artifact (e.g., Evans 1925; parts of the Aegis 
ICON volume; Vlachopoulos) and those who regard it as a probable early 
20th-century forgery by Émile Gilliéron fils (Marinatos; the 'Fakes and 
Dubitanda' chapter; discussions of dubitanda). There is also disagreement over 
its reported findspot and regional association (Thisbe/Dombrena in Boeotia vs. 
Kakovatos near Pylos), with modern authors emphasizing that both provenances 
lack secure archaeological documentation and may stem from dealer 
narratives.\n\nFor the eventual article, neutrality can be maintained by: (1) 
presenting the Ring of Nestor first as a gold signet (seal) ring with elaborate 
ritual iconography that has played a notable role in scholarship on Aegean 
religion and glyptic; (2) summarizing Evans\x19s original description of it as a
Late Mycenaean gold ring from a tomb near Thisbe, and the subsequent reuse of 
his narrative; (3) explaining that later researchers have re-evaluated both its 
provenance and its authenticity, placing it among 'dubitanda' or suspected 
forgeries; (4) clearly distinguishing between scholars who argue for 
authenticity and those who argue for forgery, attributing positions explicitly 
with citations and without endorsing either side; and (5) clearly noting that 
the ring\x19s current museum location and precise ownership history are not 
firmly documented in accessible institutional records, even though some 
secondary literature associates a \x18Ring of Nestor\x19 with the Ashmolean 
Museum. The article should avoid asserting authenticity, findspot, or present 
location as settled facts and instead frame them as debated issues within the 
scholarly literature.", 'query_id': '20260628150925202013_b4e7aeb3'})
