including number and type of figures, symbolic motifs, and their arrangement, 
specifically in the context of authenticity and coherence with Minoan/Mycenaean 
religious scenes.", "Additional iconographic and interpretive treatments: 
Vlachopoulos’ chapter includes detailed description and interpretation of the 
bezel imagery, analyzing figures, spatial setting, and ritual/religious 
significance.", "Comparative scene analysis in epiphany studies: Cain’s 2001 
article uses the Ring of Nestor as one of several epiphany-type rings, includes 
drawings of impressions, and discusses composition (figures, divine/supernatural
appearances, cult equipment) within a critique of epiphany narratives.", 
"Interpretation as sacred place: “Traces of Places: Sacred Sites in Miniature on
Minoan Gold Rings” treats the Ring of Nestor as a case study, describing a 
griffin seated on/associated with a structural element and table altars, and 
interpreting the ensemble as a constructed sacred place combining cult 
furnishings, architecture/landscape, and mythological creatures.", "Afterworld /
funerary interpretation: Evans’ classic article “The ‘Ring of Nestor’: a glimpse
into the Minoan afterworld and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings from 
Thisbe, Boeotia” (JHS 45) is cited as interpreting the imagery as a glimpse into
a Minoan afterworld and framing it within funerary treasure from Thisbe.", "Use 
in broader scholarship and teaching: the Aegis ICON volume and the Dartmouth 
Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology bibliography treat Evans’ article and subsequent 
reassessments as core references for the ring’s iconography; a Birmingham thesis
references an OJA article “Ring of Nestor” in a discussion of iconography and 
dating limits.", "Authenticity context for iconography: Vlachopoulos and the 
‘Some observations’ paper both explicitly tie iconographic analysis to debates 
over forgery/authenticity; an AJA review notes some scholars still harbor 
doubts, showing iconographic readings are framed by this controversy."], 
