### 1. Vlachopoulos 2020 – “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity” 
(Aegis 18)

- **Title**: (2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in
Aegean Iconography*, Aegis 18 (2020), 223–252  
- **URL**: 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252  
- **Author / Publisher**: Andreas G. Vlachopoulos; paper hosted on Academia.edu 
(originally in the Aegis 18 volume edited by Fritz Blakolmer).  
- **Date**: 2020 (volume year).  
- **Source type**: Scholarly book chapter (peer‑reviewed conference volume).

- **Relevance to the conflict**: This is the most detailed modern study 
explicitly devoted to the authenticity question. It documents the history of 
doubts and argues that recent research now supports authenticity.

- **What the text says (reliably extractable from the accessible portion)**:
  - The abstract states that the “many years of research that led to the present
paper on the so-called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277), **with the 
documentation of its authenticity**…” have allowed the author to discuss earlier
erroneous identifications of certain Creto-Mycenaean gold rings as fakes.[1]
  - It continues that this earlier tendency to consider rings like the Ring of 
Nestor and the Ring of Minos as forgeries “**has been reassessed completely in 
favour of their authenticity**.”[1]
  - This reassessment is said to be “significantly reinforced by **recent finds 
of iconographic subjects of comparable resonance** (wall-paintings, gold rings, 
figurines, ivories, and so on), as well as by more thorough study of the 
**techniques and craftsmanship** of these unique signet rings of the first 
centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC).”[1]

- **How it bears on the conflict**:
  - Vlachopoulos does not merely express a personal opinion; he frames a 
**field-wide revision**, describing the earlier suspicion of forgery as a 
“tendency” that has now “been reassessed completely” in favor of 
authenticity.[1]
  - He explicitly grounds this in **independent lines of evidence**: new 
comparanda (iconography) and technical/manufacturing analysis.[1]
  - This strongly supports the claim that current mainstream scholarship treats 
the ring as **authentic**, not as an unresolved case.

---

### 2. AJA Book Review of CMS VI – Acknowledging Residual Doubts

- **Title**: *Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel. Vol. 6, Oxford, the 
Ashmolean Museum*  
- **URL**: https://ajaonline.org/book-review/966  
- **Author / Publisher**: Review by Olga Krzyszkowska; *American Journal of 
Archaeology* (AJA).  
- **Date**: July 2011 (AJA 115.3).  
- **Source type**: Peer‑reviewed journal book review.

- **Relevance to the conflict**: This review discusses CMS VI, the standard 
corpus volume that includes the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277). It has been 
cited (in your upstream material) as noting that “some scholars still harbor 
doubts” about the ring’s authenticity.

- **What we can see directly**:
  - The accessible part of the review (introduction) outlines:
    - The scope and importance of CMS VI.
    - The quality and history of the collection and its documentation.
    - An appendix summarizing scientific analyses of selected pieces.[2]
  - The truncated online preview doesn’t itself show the sentence about ongoing 
doubts, but:
    - The review explicitly mentions a “valuable appendix summarizing scientific
analyses of selected pieces (31–5).”[2]
    - CMS VI is known (from other scholarly references) to include discussion 
and some technical analyses of problematic or contested pieces, a context in 
which the Ring of Nestor is normally treated.

- **How it bears on the conflict**:
  - Even without the specific sentence visible in the current excerpt, the 
review is:
    - **Later than many 20th‑century doubts** (2011).
    - Focused on CMS VI’s treatment of the Ashmolean seals, including scientific
testing.
  - In that context, the claim (reported in your existing notes) that the review
“notes that some scholars still harbor doubts” is consistent with the review 
situating the ring as **accepted into the corpus but not beyond all doubt**.
  - The review therefore likely reflects a **transitional position** (ca. 2011):
the ring is catalogued in the standard corpus and discussed with scientific 
data, but the field still acknowledges a minority of doubters.

