      "source_ref": "s6",
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st22",
      "text": "The article in the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections 
describes the Ring of Nestor as a pseudo-Minoan object and explicitly treats it 
as a forgery rather than an authentic Aegean Bronze Age artifact.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st23",
      "text": "The pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring article states that the ring was 
reportedly discovered in 1924 and accepted as genuine by Arthur Evans, but notes
that many other scholars have considered it a forgery.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "qualifiers": ["Reported discovery date and acceptance by Evans are 
presented as part of the ring’s narrative rather than fully documented 
excavation records."],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st24",
      "text": "The pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring article compares the ring’s 
iconography with Egyptian motifs and argues that its hybrid or transplanted 
elements are more consistent with a modern creation drawing on Egyptian sources 
than with authentic Bronze Age Aegean iconography.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st25",
      "text": "The pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring article reinforces the view that 
the Ring of Nestor is a modern forgery, contributing to the scholarly strand 
that rejects its authenticity.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "local_ref": "e1",
      "text": "Hudler explicitly categorizes the Ring of Nestor among Aegean 
gemmae dubitandae and notes that its authenticity has been debated, citing 
scholars such as Karo, Nilsson, Biesantz, and Xenaki-Sakellariou who questioned 
or rejected its genuineness.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e2",
      "text": "Hudler reports that Georg Karo in the early 20th century was one 
of the first to explicitly regard the Ring of Nestor as a modern forgery, 
marking the beginning of the forgery tradition associated with the ring.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e3",
      "text": "Hudler describes Martin Nilsson’s change between the first and 
second editions of Minoan-Mycenaean Religion, noting that Nilsson reclassified 
the Ring of Nestor as a suspect object under increased awareness of forgery 
issues.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e4",
      "text": "According to Hudler, Hagen Biesantz (1954) adopted a clearly 
negative stance on the authenticity of the ring, primarily on the basis of 
stylistic irregularities he observed.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e5",
      "text": "Hudler notes that Agni Xenaki-Sakellariou raised doubts about 
several imagery details of the ring and criticized its typological and technical
features, contributing to mid-20th-century skepticism.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "text": "Hudler states that Ingo Pini’s 1998 article explicitly engages 
with Xenaki-Sakellariou’s criticisms and introduces new technical and 
comparative evidence to argue that the Ring of Nestor is authentic and belongs 
to the Late Bronze Age sequence of gold seal rings.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "text": "Hudler characterizes the Ring of Nestor as a prominent example 
used to explore the position of doubtful sealstones and suspected fakes within 
Aegean Bronze Age archaeology, illustrating a long, unresolved debate.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "text": "The Met Perspectives essay summarizes Nanno Marinatos’s argument 
that the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor were forged by Émile Gilliéron 
fils, presenting them as products of the Gilliéron workshop rather than genuine 
Bronze Age rings.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": ["Secondary summary of Marinatos; exact wording and full 
argument are not reproduced."],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "text": "The Met essay discusses the Rings of Minos and Nestor within a 
broader narrative of early 20th-century reconstruction and replication, 
highlighting concerns that some Gilliéron-associated objects may be modern 
creations rather than authentic archaeological finds.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": ["Contextual, not technical, evidence."],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "The Cambridge “Fakes and Dubitanda” chapter explicitly includes 
the Ring of Nestor among objects whose authenticity has been repeatedly doubted 
and remarks that such doubts have not ceased and probably never will.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "The same chapter notes that some scholars nonetheless accept or 
use the Ring of Nestor as genuine material, setting it within a category of 
contested but still employed artifacts in Aegean Bronze Age studies.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": ["Summary; specific scholars not detailed in the 
snippet."],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "Vlachopoulos explicitly states that his 2020 article aims at 
documenting the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor, thereby framing the work as 
a pro-authenticity study.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "text": "Vlachopoulos reviews early scholarly opinions that classified 
certain Cretan-Mycenaean gold rings, including the Ring of Nestor, as forgeries,
and asserts that this view has been comprehensively re-evaluated in light of new
discoveries and technical research.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e14",
      "text": "Vlachopoulos argues that newly discovered frescoes, gold rings, 
ivories, and statuettes with related themes, together with detailed goldworking 
analyses, show that the Ring of Nestor’s techniques and iconography accord with 
16th–15th century BC Aegean practice.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": ["Specific comparative examples and metrics are not 
included in the summary."],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e15",
      "text": "Vlachopoulos identifies the Ring of Nestor as CMS VI, no. 277 and
records its dimensions as approximately 3.29 x 2.18 cm, noting it is the 
fifth-largest known Aegean signet ring.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [],
      "limitations": ["Figures taken from the described summary; full 
