[AIMessage → tool] task({'subagent_type': 'reporter', 'description': "Using the 
completed Research Graph for goal 20260628150924715457_6b42b22c, write a 
Wikipedia-style article in English about the archaeological object known as the 
'Ring of Nestor'. Follow the user-provided rubric on being well-written, broad 
in coverage, and neutral in tone. Do NOT access or use any Wikipedia pages. 
Every factual claim in the article must be supported by at least one numbered 
in-text citation in square brackets (e.g., [1], [2]). At the end of the article,
include a 'References' section that lists, in order, the full URLs corresponding
to each numbered citation used in the text.\n\nStructure the article with: (1) a
lead section (without a heading) that defines the Ring of Nestor, situates it in
time and culture, explains its significance, and briefly notes the authenticity 
debate; (2) a section on 'Description' covering physical characteristics, 
dimensions, material, and iconography; (3) a section on 'Discovery and 
provenance' summarizing traditional findspot narratives (e.g., Thisbe/Dombrena, 
Kakovatos/Pylos), antiquities-market pathways, and the lack of secure 
archaeological documentation; (4) a section on 'Dating and cultural attribution'
explaining its usual placement in the Late Bronze Age and Minoan–Mycenaean 
glyptic traditions, with relevant debates; (5) a section on 'Interpretation' 
discussing major scholarly readings of the imagery, function, and religious or 
funerary significance; (6) a section on 'Authenticity debate' neutrally 
presenting the main lines of argument that treat the ring as authentic versus 
those that consider it a modern forgery or gemma dubitanda, with clear 
attribution to specific scholars and works; and (7) a 'References' section 
listing all sources with full URLs. Where provenance or current location is 
uncertain or disputed, clearly indicate that the information is contested and 
avoid presenting any single view as established fact. Ensure the wording 
respects the 'Words to Watch' and neutrality guidance from the rubric."})
