"20260628151234004919_e0010adc", "description": "Different sources imply 
different primary find locations or regional associations for the Ring of 
Nestor: Evans’s publications place the ring in a hoard from a Mycenaean chamber 
tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas The Past article refers to it as a gold 
signet ring from the area around Pylos.", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151230534854_5c4a213f", "20260628151230451409_2f099888",
"20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"]}, {"id": "20260628151234095355_71a18dc3", 
"description": "There is a discrepancy in the chronological framing of the Ring 
of Nestor: Evans dates the hoard to the Late Mycenaean period without giving an 
explicit absolute date in the available summaries, while the UCLA event page 
approximately dates the ring to c. 1500 BC, which is generally earlier than the 
very late Mycenaean phase indicated by associated weapons in Evans’s account.", 
"conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": ["20260628151230555666_cc9caa1c", 
"20260628151230451409_2f099888", "20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"]}, {"id": 
"20260628151255689019_90e6249e", "description": "关于“Ring of 
Nestor”的出土地，若干学术来源沿用其据称来自靠近皮洛斯的 Kakovatos 
石室墓这一说法，但同时有研究指出这一出土地仅见于收藏者或市场叙述，缺乏正式考古记
录，因此被视为存疑。", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628151253053625_bd320d09", "20260628151252971610_85a3f3b7"]}, {"id": 
"20260628151422863130_4aa99b79", "description": "Different sources assign 
somewhat differing chronological ranges to the Ring of Nestor: some emphasize 
Late Minoan or early Mycenaean production (e.g., Knossian workshop), while 
others stress Late Helladic II–III or specifically Late Helladic III Mycenaean 
dating.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628151417869830_efe746da", "20260628151417848010_30659486", 
"20260628151417761251_2daa045d", "20260628151100138803_64a1bc49"]}, {"id": 
"20260628151422994137_aaa490e5", "description": "Sources differ over cultural 
attribution: some treat the Ring of Nestor primarily as a product of Minoan 
religious and artistic tradition, others emphasize Mycenaean ownership, 
deposition, and use, and several present it as a hybrid object reflecting both 
spheres.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628151417889514_883a3ee4", "20260628151417869830_efe746da", 
"20260628151417848010_30659486", "20260628151417806158_d919de49", 
"20260628151417761251_2daa045d", "20260628151100138803_64a1bc49"]}, {"id": 
"20260628151423211900_023f34ed", "description": "Interpretations of the Ring of 
Nestor’s imagery vary: Evans and followers view it as connected with a \"Minoan 
after‑world\" and funerary beliefs; other scholars emphasize cultic or epiphany 
scenes; yet others explore sacrificial or proto‑mythological readings, while 
acknowledging the possibility of generic cult imagery.", "conflict_type": 
"factual", "source_ids": ["20260628151417869830_efe746da", 
"20260628151417826918_c28066af", "20260628151417806158_d919de49", 
"20260628151417782383_dd950f89", "20260628151417761251_2daa045d"]}, {"id": 
"20260628151423392672_43f2a1a2", "description": "There is disagreement over the 
function and context of the Ring of Nestor: some stress its role as a practical 
signet used in administration, others highlight its status as an heirloom 
keimēlion; interpretations of the associated treasure range from funerary 
deposit to temple offerings to heirloom collection.", "conflict_type": 
"factual", "source_ids": ["20260628151417889514_883a3ee4", 
"20260628151417848010_30659486", "20260628151417761251_2daa045d", 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205"]}, {"id": "20260628151423518816_7bf19b63", 
"description": "Earlier scholarship questioned the authenticity of the Ring of 
Nestor, whereas later studies, such as Vlachopoulos’ metallurgical and 
contextual analysis, affirm its authenticity as a genuine Late Bronze Age 
artifact.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628151417826918_c28066af", "20260628151417761251_2daa045d", 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205"]}, {"id": "20260628152127775748_9f8d655b", 
"description": "Slight discrepancy in the stated chronological range for the 
Late Minoan I Period between Ashmolean search snippets (c. 1600–c. 1450 BC) and 
the Heritage Images record (c. 1700–c. 1450 BC).", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152126191547_5ec8fdde", 
"20260628152126156237_05dca35b"]}, {"id": "20260628152945843407_12ccd992", 
"description": "Sources differ in the stated traditional provenance of the Ring 
of Nestor catalogued as CMS VI 277: one source reports it as said to be from a 
tholos tomb in Kakovatos, while another states that it was found at the tomb of 
Nestor’s Pylos.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628152944303253_d35eab22", "20260628152944259463_6aff0bf8"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153202313139_4e8b8551", "description": "Vlachopoulos 2020 presents the 
suspicion that the Ring of Nestor is a forgery as a past scholarly tendency that
has now been completely reassessed in favor of authenticity, whereas the JAEI 
article’s title and wording foreground the concept of a \"Pseudo-Minoan\" Nestor
Ring and states that many scholars condemned it as a forgery, implying ongoing 
or renewed skepticism.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628153159455560_ab187722", "20260628151059895146_c51fb205"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153202473889_aef8101b", "description": "The convergence of 
Vlachopoulos’s claim that the ring’s authenticity has been documented and 
reassessed completely in its favor with the ResearchGate article’s statement 
that new technical observations support authenticity contrasts with the 
continued framing of the ring as problematic in the JAEI article and with 
reported remarks in the AJA review that some scholars still harbor doubts.", 
"conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": ["20260628153159384695_40853dbc", 
"20260628153159455560_ab187722", "20260628153159414254_393f717c", 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205"]}, {"id": "20260628153302060182_1eec2e23", 
"description": "Disagreement over whether the Ring of Nestor is an authentic 
Aegean Bronze Age gold signet ring or a modern forgery, with some sources 
attributing it specifically to Émile Gilliéron fils.", "conflict_type": 
"factual", "source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722", 
"20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "20260628153159414254_393f717c", 
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205", "20260628151252992632_80181a53", 
"20260628151253014064_8bb784f2", "20260628153258496458_4c1984ff"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153302431797_1ded43b7", "description": "Conflict between 
Marinatos-derived interpretations summarized by the Met essay and Vlachopoulos’s
2020 study: the former presents the Ring of Nestor as a forgery produced by 
Émile Gilliéron fils, while the latter claims to document its authenticity based
on technical and iconographic evidence.", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151059895146_c51fb205", 
"20260628151253014064_8bb784f2"]}, {"id": "20260628153302568960_f2d9686a", 
"description": "Conflict between the pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring article, which 
explicitly treats the ring as a modern pseudo-Minoan forgery with 
Egyptian-derived iconography, and studies such as Pini’s (via Hudler), the 
ResearchGate technical article, and Vlachopoulos, which argue that technical and
comparative data support authenticity.", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722", "20260628153159414254_393f717c",
"20260628151059895146_c51fb205", "20260628153258496458_4c1984ff"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153302750277_5f2ce4c8", "description": "Methodological divergence where
some scholars, like Eliopoulos and the Cambridge “Fakes and Dubitanda” chapter, 
use the Ring of Nestor cautiously for iconographic analysis while emphasizing 
its disputed status, contrasted with others who either fully accept it as 
authentic (e.g., Vlachopoulos) or categorically reject it as a forgery (e.g., 
pseudo-Minoan Nestor ring article, Marinatos).", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722", "20260628151059895146_c51fb205",
"20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "20260628151252992632_80181a53"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153912009478_6cd74e4c", "description": "关于 Nestor 
指环真实性的冲突：伊文斯 1925 年著作及相关书目信息将该指环视为来自波奥蒂亚 
Thisbë 
岩石墓葬的真实晚期迈锡尼金质印戒，并作为研究米诺斯来世世界的考古证据；而后续期刊
论文《The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian 
Iconography》则强调许多学者将其视为伪作并将其归入“伪米诺斯”类别。", 
"conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722", 
"20260628151230514647_bd0181ed", "20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"]}, {"id": 
"20260628153912150476_d53d03e1", "description": 
"在怀疑与支持之间存在进一步冲突：一方面，《The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its
Egyptian Iconography》强调学界中对该指环伪作观点的广泛存在；另一方面，Eliopoulos
的图像学分析和 ResearchGate 上《The 'Ring of 
Nestor'》文章提出新的图像学和技术观察，认为弯曲祭坛图像对应以及技术细节支持该指
环的真实性，并记录其在 1995 年再次展出的事实。", "conflict_type": "factual", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153159414254_393f717c", "20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f",
"20260628153159455560_ab187722"]}, {"id": "20260628153912281910_dec46054", 
"description": "在处理有问题出处文物的方法论上存在态度差异：Pollio 
主张不应先验排除所有未具正式出土记录的指环，认为这种排除会忽视重要数据资源；与此
形成对照的是，将 Nestor 
指环归为“伪米诺斯”并视为伪作的研究倾向于因其市场化和非受控发掘背景而质疑其真实性
。", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722",
"20260628153910176598_4eabc779"]}, {"id": "20260628154040932169_7dae0665", 
"description": "Evans’s narrative presents the Ring of Nestor as an authentic 
Mycenaean gold signet ring forming part of a sepulchral treasure from a specific
rock-cut tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas modern scholarship by Hudler and 
the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections emphasizes the ring’s lack of 
secure archaeological documentation, market-linked provenance, and stylistically
or iconographically suspicious features, classifying it as a gemma dubitanda or 
pseudo-Minoan forgery.", "conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": 
["20260628153159455560_ab187722", "20260628154038516188_12108330", 
"20260628151252971610_85a3f3b7", "20260628151230576658_79618ae4"]}, {"id": 
"20260628154041142607_d511a452", "description": "Within modern scholarship there
is disagreement over the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor: Hudler and the 
Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections stress doubts and classify the ring
among suspect or forged objects, while Ingo Pini’s study argues in favor of its 
authenticity based on comparative iconographic and stylistic analysis.", 
"conflict_type": "factual", "source_ids": ["20260628153159455560_ab187722", 
