{"query": {"id": "20260628150925291248_50a8570b", "handle": "q4", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” museum collection provenance current location", "importance": 
"supporting"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628151827796854_daaee87b", "type": 
"Evidence", "text": "The Perspectives article notes that Harvard Art Museums 
\"hold a collection of fifteen Gilliéron reproductions\" of Aegean Bronze Age 
objects, produced in the early twentieth century and circulated among 
institutions alongside other casts and watercolors created by Émile Gilliéron.",
"source_ids": ["20260628151253014064_8bb784f2"], "citations": [18]}, {"id": 
"20260628151827855246_5fa7eb1d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans’s 1925 
publication is titled \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia\" and is divided into sections on the sepulchral treasure (pp. 
1–42) and the Minoan after-world (pp. 43–75), followed by plates.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628151827914370_7a3b7ab9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "According to 
Evans’s account, the hoard of gold signet-rings and bead-seals was \"found in a 
chamber tomb cut in rock\" near the modern village of Thisbê in Boeotia in 1915 
by a local peasant, and came to his attention through private channels because 
of wartime conditions.", "source_ids": ["20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"], 
"citations": [9]}, {"id": "20260628151827980149_98250da2", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans identifies one ring from the hoard, characterized by an elaborate
engraved scene, as \"the Ring of Nestor\" and discusses its iconography in 
detail, including altars and trees, connecting these motifs to Minoan and 
Mycenaean religious conceptions.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151230426883_01ee95ad"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828054957_5d5f74be", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The JHS article 
extract describes the assemblage as \"a truly royal treasure of gold rings and 
of bead-seals in the same metal\" found in a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê in 
Boeotia, discovered in 1915 by a peasant from the village of Dombrena.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151230451409_2f099888"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828125717_e434a6af", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans notes in the
JHS communication that wartime conditions \"diverted\" the discovery of the 
treasure away from official archaeological channels, but \"a fortunate chain of 
circumstances\" enabled him to describe and exhibit the hoard, which included 
bronze spearheads and a short sword of late Mycenaean type.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151230451409_2f099888"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828190992_e1d75ae7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The JHS extract 
explains that the article focuses on \"one especially elaborate gold signet 
ring\" from the hoard, labeled the \"Ring of Nestor\", and uses its iconography 
as a window onto conceptions of the \"Minoan after-world\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628151230451409_2f099888"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828253187_19d4ed91", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
metadata for Eliopoulos’s article states that it is titled \"SOME OBSERVATIONS 
ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’\" and that it was published in 
ANNUARIO della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente
in 2013.", "source_ids": ["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": [2]}, 
{"id": "20260628151828310506_26a31926", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
abstract summarized on Academia.edu indicates that Eliopoulos’s paper \"analyzes
the iconography around the Ring of Nestor in the context of Aegean forgeries and
authenticity debates\", and mentions that \"generations of scholars\" have 
questioned the genuineness of both Nestor’s and Minos’s rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828367773_ffd82ec2", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler’s article, 
hosted in Propylaeum, discusses \"gemmae dubitandae\" and explicitly references 
the \"Ring of Nestor\" as an example, noting that such objects illustrate how 
lack of solid archaeological documentation leads to contested properties and 
authenticity.", "source_ids": ["20260628151252971610_85a3f3b7"], "citations": 
[16]}, {"id": "20260628151828432943_9dc6b4d3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Cambridge chapter snippet states: \"Like the 'Ring of Nestor', it has a 
combination of scenes (female individual on a boat, altars, sacred trees, and 
baetyls) tied to the theme of divine ...\", placing the Ring of Nestor in a 
comparative discussion of elaborate multi-scene engraved rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151252992632_80181a53"], "citations": [17]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828492889_1a2dbe17", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In the Brill 
chapter, the author remarks that \"The gold 'Ring of Nestor' and 'Ring of 
Minos', which both surfaced in the 1920s, are famous examples, generally 
believed to be fake,\" situating them among suspected forgeries in Minoan art.",
"source_ids": ["20260628151825898999_8c349455"], "citations": [29]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828550622_c76aedf2", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Brill chapter 
identifies the Ring of Nestor and Ring of Minos as \"famous examples\" in a 
discussion of forgery and the antiquities market, suggesting that they have 
become emblematic within scholarly narratives about problematic Minoan 
artifacts.", "source_ids": ["20260628151825898999_8c349455"], "citations": 
[29]}, {"id": "20260628151828619600_ccc94939", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Academia.edu listing for Vlachopoulos’s article notes that it appears in F. 
Blakolmer (ed.), \"Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography\", Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223–252, under the title \"The Ring of 
Nestor and the quest for authenticity\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628151828685065_e98666e8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The snippet from 
Vlachopoulos’s article refers to a section titled \"The provenance of the Ring 
of Nestor\" and cites Evans (1925b:46) beginning with the phrase \"On the death 
of the peasant who had the good luck to ﬁnd it, the ...\", indicating that the 
article discusses how the ring’s ownership changed after the original finder’s 
death.", "source_ids": ["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, 
{"id": "20260628151851256459_e3b673c0", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of 
Nestor belongs to a hoard of gold signet rings and bead-seals discovered in 1915
in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near modern Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, 
found by a local peasant from the nearby village of Dombrena, and came to Arthur
J. Evans through private channels because wartime conditions diverted the find 
from official archaeological oversight.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151230426883_01ee95ad", "20260628151230451409_2f099888"], "citations":
[9, 10]}, {"id": "20260628151851447058_99dfa36d", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Within the Thisbê hoard, Arthur J. Evans singled out one especially elaborate 
engraved gold signet ring, naming it the \"Ring of Nestor\" and making it the 
centerpiece of his 1925 study, in which he interpreted its iconography 
(including altars and sacred trees) as reflecting Minoan and Mycenaean religious
conceptions and a \"Minoan after-world.\"", "source_ids": 
["20260628151230426883_01ee95ad", "20260628151230451409_2f099888"], "citations":
[9, 10]}, {"id": "20260628151851604605_a4fbdec3", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Modern scholarship characterizes the Ring of Nestor as a contentious Aegean 
artifact with insecure provenance beyond its initial discovery, noting that it 
moved through private ownership after the finder’s death and that the lack of 
solid archaeological documentation has led to long‑running debates over its 
authenticity.", "source_ids": ["20260628151059895146_c51fb205", 
"20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f", "20260628151252971610_85a3f3b7"], "citations": 
[1, 2, 16]}, {"id": "20260628151851851726_6993104a", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Several modern authors situate the Ring of Nestor among Aegean forgeries and 
\"gemmae dubitandae\": a Brill chapter states that the gold Ring of Nestor and 
Ring of Minos, both surfacing in the 1920s, are famous examples generally 
believed to be fake, while Hudler and a Cambridge volume treat the Ring of 
Nestor as a paradigmatic object whose authenticity is questioned.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151252971610_85a3f3b7", "20260628151252992632_80181a53",
"20260628151825898999_8c349455"], "citations": [16, 17, 29]}, {"id": 
"20260628151852025213_b12fd020", "type": "Claim", "text": "Other recent 
scholarship, such as Eliopoulos’s study, re‑examines the Ring of Nestor’s 
iconography and technical analyses in the context of Aegean forgeries and argues
that iconographic elements (including incurved altars) support the ring’s 
authenticity, even while acknowledging generations of debate over its 
genuineness.", "source_ids": ["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": 
[2]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 20, "total": 33, "next_offset": 20}
