{"query": {"id": "20260628151934236498_3c0705b2", "handle": "q4.2", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" museum collection provenance current location A. G. 
Vlachopoulos 2020 \"The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity\" full 
text PDF \"The provenance of the Ring of Nestor\" ownership chain dealers 
institutions catalog references", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628152124891149_1eefb6f9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“(2020) A. G. 
Vlachopoulos, ‘The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity’, in F. 
Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, 
Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628152124959834_76f8b8be", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Abstract excerpt: 
“The many years of research that led to the present paper on the so-called Ring 
of Nestor (CMS VI, nο. 277), with the documentation of its authenticity, have 
offered me the opportunity to discuss the cases of erroneous identifications of 
some Creto-Mycenaean gold rings as fakes. This tendency, underpinned by the 
obscure – even legendary – circumstances in which these rings were found, not 
only the Ring of Nestor but also the Ring of Minos, has been reassessed 
completely in favour of their authenticity. This revision has been significantly
reinforced by recent finds of iconographic subjects of comparable resonance 
(wall-paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories, and so on), as well as by more 
thorough study of the techniques and craftsmanship of these unique signet rings 
of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th-15th century BC). The key 
pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor examined in this paper is Evans’s 
‘watch-dog’ or ‘dragon’, namely the guardian of the old ‘tree of the world’, 
whose branches host the most populous scene in Aegean glyptic art. This motif is
here identified as a scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Αge Aegean 
iconography, which kills the ‘Μinoan Dragon’ of the ring and offers a sound 
interpretation of the ring’s iconography.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628152125043172_15da23bc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Academia 
自动生成的“Key takeaways”条目之一写道：“The Ring of Nestor, measuring 3.29 cm by
2.18 cm, is the fifth largest Aegean signet ring.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628152125119240_fdc60999", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Academia 
页面显示描述信息：“description 36 pages”，表明该章节 PDF 为 36 页。", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151059895146_c51fb205"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628152125191131_b360cb97", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Academia 
页面中关于 Eliopoulos 论文的描述为：“SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE
‘RING OF NESTOR’ … 2013, ANNUARIO della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle 
Missioni Italiane in Oriente … description 22 pages”。", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628152125272574_4dd6f5b9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Eliopoulos 
论文在托管页面的摘要中写道：“This paper presents an analysis of the iconography 
surrounding the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates. By revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, 
the paper aims to contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's 
significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical 
scholarship and recent discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions 
related to the ring's motifs and their implications.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628152125343928_727c17f9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "托管页面自动“Key 
takeaways”之一指出：“Generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of 
both Nestor's and Minos' rings.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628151059963850_4b6ddc5f"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628152251772009_3ccf84af", "type": "Claim", "text": "A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 
