ring associated with the Late Bronze Age Aegean and commonly discussed within 
the Minoan–Mycenaean glyptic tradition.[1][8][22] Its bezel carries a complex 
multi‑scene composition combining human figures, altars, sacred trees, animals 
and other cultic elements, which has made it a key case study in the 
interpretation of Aegean ritual imagery and sacred landscapes.[2][6][17][22][24]
The ring was first presented in detail by Arthur J. Evans in 1925 as the most 
elaborate piece in a hoard of gold rings and bead‑seals said to have been found 
in a Mycenaean chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, and he interpreted its 
imagery as offering a "glimpse into the Minoan after‑world".[9][10][22] Since 
its publication, the Ring of Nestor has attracted sustained scholarly attention,
both for what it may reveal about Late Bronze Age religion and elite ideology 
and for the methodological challenges posed by its problematic provenance and 
controversial authenticity.[1][2][16][17][18][29] While some researchers regard 
the ring (catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277) as an authentic Creto‑Mycenaean 
artifact of the 16th–15th century BC, others classify it among Aegean "fakes and
dubitanda" or consider it a modern pseudo‑Minoan forgery, and the object remains
at the centre of an ongoing authenticity 
