'20260628151100022629_e8d266c5'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The 
iconography of the bezel has been analysed in detail. Studies consistently 
identify at least one seated female figure beneath or beside a tree, approaching
or adjacent male figures, one or more altars or table‑altars, sacred trees, 
baetyl‑like stones, animals and a composite creature standing before a 
tree.[1][2][6][17][22][23][24] Some descriptions also refer to a female figure 
in a boat as part of the scene, linking the composition to other multi‑scene 
rings that show women in boats in association with altars and sacred trees.[17] 
The composite creature has been variously described as a "watch‑dog" or "dragon"
and more recently re‑interpreted as a scorpion standing on a grassy ground 
before a tree whose branches extend above, functioning as a guardian of the 
sacred tree and as an apotropaic symbol.[1][24]', 'source_ids': 
